            <div class="questions-content" id="physics-questions">
    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-number">It’s raining conductors!</div>
        <div class="question-text">
            We have a large circle of radius <strong>R = 1.1 m</strong>. A point charge A of charge <strong>Q = 32&nbsp;µC</strong> is placed on the circumference of the circle.
            <br><br>
            Neutral conducting points B, C, D, ... fall one by one at the center of the circle. As soon as the <em>n<sup>th</sup></em> point arrives, it is made to touch all pre-existing points in alphabetical order — first it touches A, then B, then C, and so on.
            <br><br>
            After redistribution of charge, it is placed on the circumference, and the system is externally arranged into a regular <strong>n-sided polygon</strong> inscribed in the circle.
            <br><br>
            Then the <em>(n + 1)<sup>th</sup></em> point falls, touches the existing <em>n</em> points in order, and the configuration is rearranged into a regular <strong>(n + 1)-sided polygon</strong>.
            <br><br>
            Let <strong>U<sub>n</sub></strong> denote the total electrostatic potential energy of the system when an <em>n</em>-sided polygon is formed.
            <br><br>
            <strong>Find U<sub>4</sub> to the nearest integer.</strong>
            <br><br>
            <em>Assume:</em> Neglect induction during conduction. Use the constant \( \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0} = 9 \times 10^9 \, \text{N·m}^2/\text{C}^2 \).
        </div>
    </div>

                    
                <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-number">Sherlock Holmes</div>
        <div class="question-text">
            In the Sherlock Holmes story <em>“The Adventure of the Dying Detective”</em>, we come across an ivory box containing a sharp spring compressed by the lid of the box. On opening, the spring — uniformly overloaded with deadly microbes — elongates and pricks the hand of Victor Savage, who dies after 4 days.
            <br><br>
            Let the box be cubical with edge length <strong>a = 10 cm</strong>. The natural (unstretched) length of the spring is <strong>ℓ = 10.2 cm</strong>. Consider the sharp end of the spring to be conical, with a <strong>semi-vertical angle α = 15°</strong>.
            <br><br>
            Suppose the microbes infect Red Blood Corpuscles (RBCs), and the infected RBCs lose their oxygen-carrying capacity. The number of infected RBCs at time <em>t</em> follows:
            <br>
            <strong>N(t) = N<sub>0</sub> e<sup>λt</sup></strong>, 
            <br>
            where <strong>λ = 1.2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> s<sup>−1</sup></strong>, and <strong>N<sub>0</sub></strong> is the initial number of microbes that enter the bloodstream.
            <br><br>
            A person dies when the number of infected RBCs reaches <strong>60%</strong> of the original RBC count in their body.
            <br><br>
            Assume:
            <ul>
                <li>Total blood volume of Victor: <strong>V = 5 L</strong></li>
                <li>RBC concentration: <strong>β = 5 × 10<sup>6</sup> per mL</strong></li>
                <li>Victor died exactly after <strong>4 days</strong></li>
                <li>Surface density of microbes on the conical tip = <strong>σ × 10<sup>12</sup>/m²</strong></li>
                <li>Neglect any resistive force to the spring’s motion</li>
                <li>Assume all microbes that pierce the skin reach the bloodstream</li>
            </ul>
            <strong>Find the value of σ.</strong>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="question-card">
    <div class="question-number">Keep chasing your partner!</div>
    <div class="question-text">
        Three people <strong>P<sub>1</sub></strong>, <strong>P<sub>2</sub></strong>, and <strong>P<sub>3</sub></strong> are standing at the vertices A(0, 0), B(0, 3), and C(4, 0) of a right triangle ABC, respectively.
        <br><br>
        At time <strong>t = 0</strong>, they start moving such that:
        <ul>
            <li><strong>P<sub>1</sub></strong>'s velocity vector is always aimed at <strong>P<sub>3</sub></strong></li>
            <li><strong>P<sub>3</sub></strong>'s velocity vector is always aimed at <strong>P<sub>2</sub></strong></li>
            <li><strong>P<sub>2</sub></strong>'s velocity vector is always aimed at <strong>P<sub>1</sub></strong></li>
        </ul>
        Their speeds are such that they meet each other simultaneously, and the triangle formed by them retains its shape throughout the motion.
        <br><br>
        Let <strong>Q(h, k)</strong> be the point where they all meet.
        <br><br>
        If \( h + k = \frac{p}{q} \), where \( \gcd(p, q) = 1 \), and \( p, q \in \mathbb{N} \), <strong>find the value of \( p + q \)</strong>.
    </div>
</div>

    </div>
    

            <div class="questions-content hidden" id="math-questions">
                <div class="question-card">
                    <div class="question-number">Chess Check</div>
                    <div class="question-text">Let’s assume a standard 8 × 8 chess board where a white knight is at F3 square. Can it give the black
king at E8 square a check after 20 moves if the king is fixed at its position? (A move is only considered
when the knight moves)
</div>
                    
                </div>
                <div class="question-card">
                    <div class="question-number">Inverses as more inverses?</div>
                    <div class="question-text">Can \(\frac{1}{69420} \) be written as a sum of more than 42069 fractions with numerator one and denominator a
positive integer such that all the denominators are not prime?
</div>
</div>
<div class="question-card">
                    <div class="question-number">Can an Ant eat Taco?</div>
                    <div class="question-text">An ant moves from (x, y) to (y, x) or (x − 69, y − 420) or (420 − x, 69 − y). Suppose ant starts from his
home (0, 0), can he ever reach taco bell located at (12, −45)?

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
